course: dictionary
lesson: 7366
title: Pleasingly: showing something is nice
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: Pleasingly is an adverb. It tells you how something is, and it comes from pleasure, so it means in a way that gives a good feeling. «The cake was pleasingly soft.» The word soft tells you what the cake was like, and pleasingly tells you that the softness was nice to feel or taste.
explain: Pleasingly is a slightly formal, writer's word. In speech, people usually say nicely or really instead. Writers use it in reports and descriptions: «The shapes were pleasingly symmetrical», which means both halves matched in a satisfying way.
explain: To check your meaning, swap in a simpler word. If nicely fits, your meaning is close. «The room was pleasingly warm» means the warmth was nice and comfortable, not too hot and not too cold.
fact: Pleasingly is an adverb, so it tells you more about an adjective like soft, warm or simple.
fact: In its first sense, «pleasingly» means in a way that gives pleasure, satisfaction or a good feeling.
fact: In a more formal sense, «pleasingly» means pleasantly or to a satisfying degree, as in «pleasingly warm».
fact: Other phrases you may meet are «pleasingly simple» and «pleasingly symmetrical», where both sides match.
fact: «Pleasingly plump» is an old-fashioned phrase for something rounded in a nice-looking way, so be careful using it about people.
exercise: «The cake was pleasingly soft.» What does pleasingly tell us about the softness? | It was hard and dry | It was cold and wet | *It was nice to feel or taste | It was burnt
exercise: «The shapes were pleasingly symmetrical.» Which word could take the place of pleasingly? | quickly | sadly | loudly | *nicely
exercise: Mia says the room was pleasingly warm. What does she mean? | *It was warm in a nice, comfortable way | It was too hot to bear | It was dark and damp | It was freezing
test: «The soup tasted pleasingly salty.» Which word is closest in meaning to pleasingly here? | sadly | *nicely | painfully | rudely
test: Where would a writer most likely use the word pleasingly? | *In a formal report praising a good piece of work | In a quick text message saying lol | In a whispered secret | In a shouted argument
exam: «The bridge was pleasingly simple, with just one arch.» What does pleasingly mean here? | so sadly | in a rude way | *in a satisfying, nice way | very quickly
